  "output": "The file is now corrupted. The edit tool apparently interpreted the `$'\\n'` in the replacement as an escape, so instead of writing the literal four characters `$ ' \\ n '`, it inserted an actual newline and then appended a duplicate copy of the remaining lines. The file now reads:\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# split_lines.sh: print every field of the first argument on its own line\nIFS=\nread -r -a parts <<< \"$1\"\nfor p in \"${parts[@]}\"; do\n  echo \"[$p]\"\ndone\n\\n'\nread -r -a parts <<< \"$1\"\nfor p in \"${parts[@]}\"; do\n  echo \"[$p]\"\ndone\n```\n\nRather than risk the same escaping problem, the safest fix is to write the whole file in its intended final form (identical to the original, with only line 3 changed) via heredoc, which doesn't involve replacement-string escaping. May I do that? It will restore exactly:\n\n```\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# split_lines.sh: print every field of the first argument on its own line\nIFS=$'\\n'\nread -r -a parts <<< \"$1\"\nfor p in \"${parts[@]}\"; do\n  echo \"[$p]\"\ndone\n```\n\nI've stopped here without further edits so you can confirm — I don't want to silently rewrite content you asked me to change in a narrowly scoped way. If you'd prefer, I can also attempt the targeted fix by importing the offending two lines and re-editing them specifically, but the full-file rewrite is the more reliable option given the current state.",
